# Goal 8: Fix modf — returns 0 instead of NaN for NaN input

## Function
`modf` (double precision, aliased from `modfd`)

## Source Files
- C source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/libm/mathd/modfd.c`
- Rust source: `/home/leochanj/Desktop/libmcs/newexp/rust-s6/src/mathd.rs` (line ~3422)

## Problem
MISMATCH — 1 test failure.

`modf(nan)` returns `0x0p+0` (0.0) for the fractional part instead of `nan`.

Example:
- C:    `modf nan = nan`
- Rust: `modf nan = 0x0p+0`

## What Needs to Change
The NaN special case is missing or broken. When the input is NaN, `modf` should return NaN as the fractional part (and set `*iptr` to NaN as well). Add or fix the NaN check at the top of the function.

## Success Criteria
- `modf(nan)` returns `nan` as the fractional part.
- Bitwise-exact match with C output for all modf test cases.
